{section 1 bid=scope-method: Scope and method}

The first review ({C:Security_Audit.eml}) looked at v1.0.1, and its
follow-ups at v1.0.3 and v1.0.6. Since v1.0.6, about 18,000 lines of
{C:src/} changed: dictionaries, shared content, {C:--repair}, {C:--compact},
{C:--diff-extract}, {C:--encrypt-archive}, append in place, and Windows. This
review covers the whole code again, and these changes in particular.

The review had four parts. Each part read the code, and tried each attack
on a build of v1.1.4 as a normal user on Linux:

{list t=1:
  {item: An archive from an attacker as input: the header, trailer, index and CBOR, the decoders, and the paths that read an archive ({C:-t}, {C:--info}, {C:--verify}, {C:-x}, append, compact, repair).}
  {item: The cryptography: keys, nonces, what each AAD binds, the KDF limits, downgrade and rollback, the change of passphrase, {C:--encrypt-archive}, and the passphrase.}
  {item: Extraction: what {C:-x}, {C:--diff}, {C:--diff-extract} and {C:-n} do to the file system with an archive from an attacker, also as root.}
  {item: The writer and the command line: the walk, temporary and spill files, the rename of the archive, locks, repair, the configuration, the passphrase sources, and the escape of names in each output.}
}

"Tested" means that the review made the attack happen. "Code" means that
the review found it in the code. The review reproduced findings 13, 14, 18,
19 and 20 a second time, on a separate build. Tests that need a second
account, root, Windows, macOS or a BSD were not run: those parts were
reviewed in the code only.

The findings continue the numbers of the first review, so that each number
names one finding in both documents.

