Three findings bypass a fix of the first review. Finding 13 reaches the
destination as finding 2 did. Findings 15, 16 and 17 go round the rule of
finding 4, and finding 16 needs no race. Findings 25 and 38 are gaps in the
fix of finding 12.

Findings 13 to 17 matter most when root extracts an archive from someone
else, restores a tree where other users own directories, or writes an
archive in a directory that other users can write. Finding 19 affects any
user who opens a plain archive from someone else. Finding 18 needs no
attacker: a user who forgets {C:-e} loses the confidentiality of the whole
archive.

{section 1 bid=findings: Findings}

{section 2 nc bid=finding-13: Finding 13. A directory and a link to . with one path change the destination (high)}

{b:Where:} {C:finishDir} and {C:extractNode} in
{C bc=/._:src/internal/archive/extract.go}, and {C:Index.Validate} in
{C bc=/._:src/internal/format/index.go}. Tested.

{b:Problem.} The index accepts two live members with one path. An archive
holds a directory {C:x} (mode 0777, its times, {C:user.*} attributes, an
owner) and a symbolic link {C:x -> .}, in either order. Phase 1 makes both,
and the link takes the place of the empty directory. The last phase opens
{C:x} by its path, follows the link, and applies the metadata of the
directory to the destination itself. {C:a/x -> ..} does the same one level
down. The fix of finding 2 removes a member {C:.}, but not a member whose
path leads to the destination.

{b:Attack.} A user runs {C:eictar -xf evil.ect -d ~}. The home directory
becomes 0777 less the umask, its times and its {C:user.*} attributes change,
and the run ends with exit 0. With {C:-p}, the mode is exactly 0777, or 03777.
As root there is no umask, and {C:--preserve-owner} gives the destination to
the uid that the archive names. With {C:-d /} that is {C:/}. On a file system
that ignores case, {C:X} and {C:x} are enough, so a check for equal paths is
not a full fix.

{b:Evidence.} A destination of mode 0700 became 0775, with mtime 1970-01-01
and {C:user.audit="pwned"}, exit 0. The review reproduced this on a separate
build.

{b:Fix.} In the last phase, apply metadata only to the directory that phase
1 made or found: record its device and inode, and refuse a link at the last
name ({C:Lstat}, then compare with the open directory). Never apply metadata
to the destination itself. Set the times through the open descriptor. When a
directory member meets a link on disk, replace the link (as GNU tar does) or
refuse the member. The fix of finding 14 also closes this one.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-14: Finding 14. Extraction follows a link that another user put inside the destination (medium)}

{b:Where:} each operation of {C bc=/._:src/internal/archive/extract.go}
through {C:os.Root}: {C:fileWorker} ({C:OpenRoot}), {C:MkdirAll}, {C:parent},
{C:writeSymlink}, {C:applyTimes}, {C:extractHardlink} and {C:finishDir}. Tested.

{b:Problem.} {C:os.Root} refuses a path that leaves the destination, but it
follows a relative link that stays inside it. Design
{ref t=design.html#symbolic-links:§7.6} says so, and calls the risk small
when extraction goes into a new directory. But design
{ref t=design.html#extraction-reads-disk:§14.3} says "Extraction never
follows a link and never goes through a linked directory". That is true only
for a link that leads out.

{b:Attack.} Root restores {C:/home} from a backup. The backup holds
{C:alice/.ssh2/authorized_keys}, a file of alice with her key. Before the
restore, alice runs {C:ln -s ../bob/.ssh ~/.ssh2}. The restore writes her
file over {C:bob/.ssh/authorized_keys}, and gives bob's {C:.ssh} the mode of
her directory. Alice can then log in as bob. With {C:-d /}, the same reaches
{C:/root/.ssh} and {C:/etc/cron.d}. A race does the same with no link before
the run: phase 1 makes all the directories, and phase 2 opens them again by
path, so alice can swap a new directory under her home for a link.

{b:Evidence.} With the planted link, bob's {C:authorized_keys} held alice's
key, and his {C:.ssh} went from 0700 to 0775, exit 0. The review reproduced
this on a separate build. The race worked in 3 runs of 3.

{b:Fix.} Resolve each member path with no link following: on Linux 5.6 and
later, {C:openat2} with {C:RESOLVE_NO_SYMLINKS} and {C:RESOLVE_BENEATH};
elsewhere, open one name at a time with {C:O_NOFOLLOW} and {C:O_DIRECTORY}
from the destination descriptor, and do the last operation with an {C:*at}
call. Keep {C:os.Root} as a second barrier. Until then, correct design
{ref t=design.html#extraction-reads-disk:§14.3}, and warn against an
extraction as root into a tree where other users own directories.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-15: Finding 15. A race between the check of -f and its resolution (medium)}

{b:Where:} {C:createTarget} in {C bc=/._:src/internal/archive/writer.go}
({C:checkLinks}, then {C:filepath.EvalSymlinks}), and the same order in
{C:CompactArchive} in {C bc=/._:src/internal/archive/mutate.go}. Tested.

{b:Problem.} {C:checkLinks} applies the rule of {C:fs.protected_symlinks} to
each part of the path. When the path does not exist yet, it finds no link.
Then {C:EvalSymlinks} resolves the path a second time. A link that appears
between the two calls is followed with no check, and the rename replaces
its target.

{b:Attack.} The attack of {ref t=Security_Audit.html#finding-4:finding 4}:
root runs {C:eictar -cf /tmp/backup.ect} from cron. A local user makes and
removes the link {C:/tmp/backup.ect -> /etc/passwd} in a loop. A failed try
gives exit 3 or a normal create, so the user can try at each run.

{b:Evidence.} A test with the rule of another user's link, and a goroutine
that made and removed the link, replaced the victim file in each of 6 runs,
after 2 to 14 creates.

{b:Fix.} Resolve the path in one pass: {C:Lstat} each part, apply the rule
to each link, read it and go on with its target, to at most 40 links. Do not
call {C:EvalSymlinks}. Then open the parent of the target, and do the open of
the old file, the create of the temporary file and the rename relative to
that directory descriptor.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-16: Finding 16. The check of -f does not look at a link inside the target of a link (medium)}

{b:Where:} {C:checkLinks} in {C bc=/._:src/internal/archive/writer.go}, used
by create, compact, the change of passphrase and {C:--encrypt-archive}. Tested.

{b:Problem.} {C:checkLinks} checks only the parts of the path as the user
typed it. When a part is a link that it can follow, it does not check the
links in the target of that link. {C:EvalSymlinks} follows them all. The
kernel applies its rule to each link that it follows.

{b:Attack.} Root has its own link {C:~/backups/latest.ect -> /tmp/latest.ect},
and {C:/tmp/latest.ect} does not exist. A local user puts the link
{C:/tmp/latest.ect -> /etc/shadow}. Then {C:eictar -cf ~/backups/latest.ect}
by root replaces {C:/etc/shadow}. No race is necessary.

{b:Evidence.} {C:-f} on the planted link was refused; {C:-f} on the user's
own link to the planted link replaced the victim file. The review confirmed
the gap in the code.

{b:Fix.} The one-pass resolution of finding 15, which applies the rule to
every link that it follows, also inside a link target.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-17: Finding 17. Create gives the new archive the mode of a file that another user put at -f (medium)}

{b:Where:} {C:createTarget} and {C:createTemp} in
{C bc=/._:src/internal/archive/writer.go}. Tested (the mode); Code (the
owner).

{b:Problem.} The new archive takes the permission bits of the file that it
replaces, and the umask does not limit them. The owner of that file is not
checked.

{b:Attack.} A local user creates {C:/tmp/backup.ect} with mode 0666 before
root's {C:eictar -cf /tmp/backup.ect}. Root can rename over the file of
another user. The new archive, owned by root, has mode 0666: the user can
read a plaintext backup, and change or cut any backup. Under
{C:fs.protected_regular}, {C:tar} gets {C:EACCES} for this file. In a shared
group directory, the same lets one member widen the archive of another.

{b:Evidence.} A file of mode 0666 at {C:-f}, then {C:umask 077} and a create:
the archive had mode 0666.

{b:Fix.} Take the mode only from a file that the caller owns. In a sticky
directory that everyone can write, refuse a file that neither the caller nor
the owner of the directory owns (exit 3), as the kernel does. In other cases,
use {C:0666} less the umask.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-18: Finding 18. -c with a passphrase source and no -e writes a plaintext archive (medium)}

{b:Where:} {C:runCreate} in {C bc=/._:src/internal/cli/ops.go}, and
{C:validate} in {C bc=/._:src/internal/cli/options.go}. Tested.

{b:Problem.} On create, the program reads the passphrase source only with
{C:-e}. Without {C:-e}, {C:--passphrase-file} and {C:--passphrase-env} are
ignored with no message. Design {ref t=design.html#encryption:§10.3} says
"A passphrase source means 'this archive is encrypted'", and
{C:--encrypt-index} without {C:-e} is already a usage error.

{b:Attack.} No attacker is necessary. A user puts
{C:eictar -cf /mnt/offsite/backup.ect --passphrase-file /root/key /home} in
cron and forgets {C:-e}. Each run writes the whole tree in the clear to
off-site storage, with exit 0. The next read with the passphrase file says
"the archive is not encrypted; if it should be, it has been replaced or
stripped", which points to an attack and not to the missing option.

{b:Evidence.} A create with {C:--passphrase-file} and no {C:-e}: exit 0, no
output, and a later list with the passphrase file gave exit 3 with the
message above. The review reproduced this on a separate build.

{b:Fix.} On {C:-c} (and {C:-n -c}), a passphrase source without {C:-e} is a
usage error (exit 2). Do not make it imply {C:-e}: an explicit error is the
same as for the other options.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-19: Finding 19. A 7 KB plain archive makes a listing use 4 GB of memory (medium)}

{b:Where:} {C:CodecSpec.Params} ({C:map[string]any}) in
{C bc=/._:src/internal/format/index.go}, and {C:decModeFor} in
{C bc=/._:src/internal/format/cbor.go}. Tested.

{b:Problem.} The decoder accepts any CBOR value in a codec parameter, also
arrays and maps nested 32 levels deep. The bound on elements applies to
each array, not to the whole index. The bound of
{ref t=Security_Audit.html#finding-6:finding 6} limits the bytes of the
index (200 times its compressed size, at most 1 GiB), not the Go memory that
those bytes decode into. An empty CBOR map is 1 byte, and becomes about 67
bytes of Go memory. {C:Validate} does not look at the parameters.

{b:Attack.} A plain archive has one catalog entry that no member uses, with
parameters that hold 15 arrays of 4,194,304 empty maps. The 63 MB of CBOR
compresses to 7 KB. Each user who lists, extracts, verifies, appends to,
compacts or asks {C:--info} of the file pays the memory. The 1 GiB bound
allows about 67 GiB, so the kernel can stop the process, and other
processes. An encrypted index cannot be forged without the passphrase.

{b:Evidence.}

{stable col=4 header sb b== i=3% w=94%:
| Archive | Command | Peak memory | Exit |
| 7,083 bytes, empty maps | {C:-t} | 4.2 GB | 0 |
| the same | {C:--info} | 8.1 GB | 0 |
| the same | {C:-x} | 4.2 GB | 0 |
| 2,895 bytes, 4M members | {C:-t} | 1.6 GB | 3, after the decode |
}

The review reproduced the first row on a separate build: 4,189,272 KB, exit 0.

{b:Fix.} Decode a parameter only as an integer or a text string, as every
writer of eictar makes them, and refuse arrays, maps and byte strings. Bound
the number of keys and of catalog entries. Bound the decoded memory, not only
the bytes: count the items of the CBOR before the decode, and weight each
array of structures by the Go size of its element. Add the bound to design
{ref t=design.html#extraction-reads-disk:§14.3}.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-20: Finding 20. --repair removes a complete generation when its trailer magic is damaged (low)}

{b:Where:} {C:RepairArchive} in {C bc=/._:src/internal/archive/mutate.go}.
Tested.

{b:Problem.} v1.1.4 made {C:--repair} refuse to remove a generation that
looks complete, unless {C:--discard-damaged} is given
({ref t=design.html#repair:§9.5}). The test of "complete" is the 8 bytes of
the trailer magic alone. When the damage is in those bytes, repair takes the
file for an interrupted write, and removes the newest committed generation
with no question. Its index and the other 88 bytes of its trailer are good.

{b:Attack.} Not an attack: one bad byte in the last sector loses a committed
generation, in the path that v1.1.4 made safe.

{b:Evidence.} A create, an append, then one byte changed at the start of the
last trailer: {C:--repair} printed "removed 389 bytes; restored generation 1"
with exit 0, and the appended member was gone. The same damage 76 bytes
later was refused with exit 3, as intended. The review reproduced this on a
separate build.

{b:Fix.} Also take the generation as complete when the last 96 bytes, with
the magic left out, read as a trailer whose index ends at the trailer and
whose generation follows the one before. Better, offer to write the magic
again.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-21: Finding 21. Blobs that overlap (low)}

{b:Where:} {C:validateMemberRanges} in
{C bc=/._:src/internal/archive/reader.go}, {C:copyBlobs} and {C:deadSpace}
in {C bc=/._:src/internal/archive/mutate.go}, and pass B of
{C bc=/._:src/internal/archive/encrypt.go}. Tested.

{b:Problem.} Each blob must be inside the body, but two blobs can overlap. No
writer of eictar makes that: shared content uses {C:data}, and a hardlink has
no blob.

{b:Attack.} A plain archive of 1 MB holds one blob and 200 members that all
point to it. {C:--info} shows a dead space of -208,666,624 bytes, and
{C:--compact} (or {C:--encrypt-archive}) writes 210 MB. The output grows by
about the blob size for each 4.5 bytes of index.

{b:Fix.} In {C:validateMemberRanges}, sort the blobs and dictionaries by
offset, and refuse an overlap as a corrupt index. Apply the same rule in the
second reader, and state it in format.md §8. Treat a negative dead space
as damage.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-22: Finding 22. A member id of 2^64-1 wraps the next id to 0 (low)}

{b:Where:} {C:OpenAppend} in {C bc=/._:src/internal/archive/writer.go}
({C:maxID + 1}), and {C bc=/._:src/internal/archive/dedup.go}. Tested.

{b:Problem.} With an old member of id 2{S:64}-1, the first new id is 0. Dedup
then counts each old owner as written by this run, so it shares old content
without the check that design {ref t=design.html#identical-content-m11:§4.3}
describes ("an old owner is checked before it is first used").

{b:Attack.} A plain archive holds a member with id 2{S:64}-1, and a member
whose digest is that of a file the victim will append, with other bytes in
its blob. The victim's append shares the bad blob, and ends with exit 0.
{C:--verify} and extraction find it later with exit 3. With a normal id, the
same append warns and stores the file in full.

{b:Fix.} In {C:Validate}, refuse an id above a bound such as 2{S:63}. In
{C:OpenAppend}, refuse an id that wraps. In dedup, keep the set of the old
ids, and do not compare ids.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-23: Finding 23. --repair takes quadratic time on crafted trailers (low)}

{b:Where:} the scan of {C:RepairArchive} in
{C bc=/._:src/internal/archive/mutate.go}, which calls {C:Reader.load} for
each match of the trailer magic. Tested.

{b:Problem.} Each candidate trailer with a correct CRC can claim the index
from the body to its own position. The scan reads and hashes that index
before it compares the digest, so the work is about {i:n}{S:2}/192 bytes of
BLAKE3 for a file of {i:n} bytes.

{b:Attack.} {C:--repair} or {C:--repair -n} of a file from someone else.
Memory stays small. The user can stop the run.

{b:Evidence.} 1.9 MB took 5 s, and 3.8 MB took 15 s (65 s of CPU). Each
doubling of the size made it 3 to 4 times slower.

{b:Fix.} Give the scan a budget of bytes to hash, for example 4 times the
file size plus 1 GiB, and stop with exit 3 when it is used. Do the cheap
checks first: the index must end at the trailer.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-24: Finding 24. Large chunks exceed --memory-limit (low)}

{b:Where:} {C:boundByMemory} in {C bc=/._:src/internal/archive/extract.go},
and the xz decoder in {C bc=/._:src/internal/codec/xz.go}. Tested.

{b:Problem.} The bound counts two buffers of the chunk size for each worker.
xz makes a third, a dictionary of the chunk size for each chunk. No
{C:GOMEMLIMIT} is set, so the garbage of each chunk stays until the heap
doubles. The chunk size comes from the index, up to 256 MiB.

{b:Evidence.} {C:--verify --memory-limit 512M} reached 806 MB with zstd and
1.35 GB with xz, on archives of 263 KB and 1.3 MB.

{b:Fix.} Give each codec a {C:DecodeMemory(chunk)}, as {C:EncodeMemory} does
for the writer, and count it. Keep the xz dictionary in the decoder. Call
{C:debug.SetMemoryLimit} when {C:--memory-limit} is given.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-25: Finding 25. --info writes codec names and parameters with no escape (low)}

{b:Where:} {C:writeInfo} in {C bc=/._:src/internal/cli/mutate.go}. Tested.
Two parts of this review found it separately.

{b:Problem.} The fix of {ref t=Security_Audit.html#finding-12:finding 12}
escapes names on a terminal. The listing escapes the codec cell too. But
{C:--info} writes each catalog entry as it is, also an entry that no member
uses, and {C:Validate} does not check catalog names.

{b:Attack.} A catalog entry named
{C:zstd\\x1b[2J\\x1b]0;pwned\\x07}: {C:eictar --info -f evil.ect} on a terminal
clears the screen and sets the window title.

{b:Fix.} Pass each string from the archive through {C:namesFor(stdout)} in
{C:writeInfo}, with a test like {C:TestNamesOnATerminal}. Refuse a catalog name
that is not a known codec, and a parameter that is not printable.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-26: Finding 26. An append to a substituted plaintext archive stores new content in the clear (low)}

{b:Where:} {C:OpenAppend} in {C bc=/._:src/internal/archive/writer.go}, and
the option rules in {C bc=/._:src/internal/cli/options.go}. Tested.

{b:Problem.} Design {ref t=design.html#integrity-its-limits:§14.4} documents
the downgrade for reads: without a passphrase option, a plaintext archive in
place of an encrypted one opens with no prompt. A write then stores new
content in the clear. A user at a terminal cannot ask for the check: only a
passphrase file or variable turns it on, and {C:-e} on {C:-r} is a usage
error.

{b:Attack.} An attacker who can write the archive file, but has no
passphrase, puts any plaintext archive in its place. The user runs
{C:eictar -rf backup.ect new/} as usual. No prompt comes, the run ends with
exit 0, and {C:new/} is in the clear.

{b:Fix.} Accept {C:-e} on each operation on an existing archive, as "this
archive must be encrypted": prompt as now, and refuse a plaintext archive.
A configuration key such as {C:require-encryption} lets a user set it once.
Document the case in {ref t=design.html#integrity-its-limits:§14.4} and in
the man page.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-27: Finding 27. With --encrypt-index, the trailers show counts and sizes for each generation (low)}

{b:Where:} the trailer ({C bc=/._:src/internal/format/trailer.go}) and the
append path of {C bc=/._:src/internal/archive/writer.go}. Tested.

{b:Problem.} Design {ref t=design.html#what-observer-sees:§14.1} says that
with {C:--encrypt-index} "an observer sees only that the archive exists, and
its total size". But each trailer shows, in the clear, the generation, the
offset and length of the index, and the live member count. An append writes
after the old trailer, so each earlier trailer stays until a compact.

{b:Attack.} An observer with no passphrase reads, for each generation, the
number of live members, how many were added or removed, and the bytes that
it added. When a generation adds one file, its stored size is exact.

{b:Fix.} Correct design {ref t=design.html#what-observer-sees:§14.1}, the man
page and the README. A sealed member count is possible in a later format
version, but the old trailers of an append stay in the clear until a
compact.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-28: Finding 28. With --encrypt-index, the length of the index lets an attacker test a name (low)}

{b:Where:} the seal of the index in {C bc=/._:src/internal/format/index.go}:
compression, then the seal, with no padding. Tested.

{b:Problem.} The trailer gives the exact length of the sealed index
(finding 27). The index is compressed before it is sealed, so a name that
repeats part of another name makes the index shorter. This is the setting of
{ref t=Security_Audit.html#finding-7:finding 7}, for names in place of
content. Design {ref t=design.html#what-observer-sees:§14.1} documents it for
content only.

{b:Attack.} A user who can add files to the tree, and see the archive after
each backup, adds a file whose name is a guess of a secret name. In a test,
the right guess made the sealed index 7 to 8 bytes shorter at the median of
six runs.

{b:Fix.} Document it in {ref t=design.html#what-observer-sees:§14.1}. In code,
pad the sealed index to a bucket size, or do not compress a sealed index.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-29: Finding 29. Create keeps the group bits, but not the group (low)}

{b:Where:} {C:createTemp} in {C bc=/._:src/internal/archive/writer.go}. Tested.

{b:Problem.} The new archive gets the mode bits of the old one, but its group
is the primary group of the user who creates it. Compact keeps the group.

{b:Attack.} An archive is {C:-rw-r----- user:backup}. The user creates it
again, and the new file is {C:-rw-r----- user:users}. Every member of
{C:users} can then read it. No attacker acts. The user does not see the
change.

{b:Fix.} Do as compact does: keep the old group when the system permits it,
and clear the group bits and warn when it does not.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-30: Finding 30. The temporary file of create is wider than the old archive for a short time (low)}

{b:Where:} {C:createTemp} in {C bc=/._:src/internal/archive/writer.go}. Tested.

{b:Problem.} The temporary file is made with mode 0666 less the umask, and
gets the mode of the old archive after that. When the old archive is 0600
and the umask is 022, the file is 0644 for a short time. A descriptor opened
in that time can read the whole new archive later.

{b:Evidence.} A watcher with inotify opened the file before its {C:chmod} in
10 of 2000 creates.

{b:Fix.} Make the temporary file with mode 0600, and set the final mode at
the end, as compact does.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-31: Finding 31. A failed write of the output gives exit 0 (low)}

{b:Where:} the output of {C:-t}, {C:-tv}, {C:--info}, the summary of
{C:--verify} and the list of {C:-n}, in {C bc=/._:src/internal/cli/ops.go},
{C bc=/._:src/internal/cli/mutate.go} and {C bc=/._:src/internal/cli/dryrun.go}.
Tested.

{b:Problem.} These paths ignore the error of each write. {C:--json} checks
it and gives exit 4.

{b:Attack.} A script runs {C:eictar -tf a.ect > manifest} on a full disk. It
gets a short manifest and exit 0, and keeps it as correct.

{b:Fix.} Write the output through a {C:bufio.Writer}, and give exit 4 when its
{C:Flush} fails.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-32: Finding 32. The check of a passphrase file does not look at its write bits or owner (low)}

{b:Where:} {C:passphraseFileExposure} in
{C bc=/._:src/internal/cli/access_unix.go}. Tested (the mode); Code (the
owner).

{b:Problem.} The fix of {ref t=Security_Audit.html#finding-8:finding 8} warns
when others can read the file. On Unix it does not warn when others can
write it, or when another user owns it. On Windows, another owner gives a
warning.

{b:Attack.} Root encrypts backups with a passphrase file of mode 0622, or one
that another user owns. That user writes a passphrase of their own into it
before the run, and can then read the backups.

{b:Fix.} Also warn when the file is writable by group or others, or owned by
a user other than the caller or root. Check the open file, so that the check
and the read are of the same file.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-33: Finding 33. Windows: x\.. names the destination (low)}

{b:Where:} {C:IsStoredPath} in {C bc=/._:src/internal/fsutil/path.go}, and
{C:os.Root} on Windows. Code only.

{b:Problem.} {C:IsStoredPath} splits a path only on {C:/}. On Windows,
{C:os.Root} cleans {C:x\\..} to {C:.}, and removes trailing dots and spaces.
So a directory member {C:x\\..} names the destination, and {C:f}, {C:f.},
{C:F} and {C:a\\..\\f} are one file. {C:os.Root} still refuses {C::},
reserved names and a path that escapes, so nothing leaves the destination.

{b:Attack.} An archive changes the times and the read-only attribute of the
destination, and goes round the rules on collisions. Windows restores no
owner, mode or xattrs, so the effect is small.

{b:Fix.} On Windows, refuse a member path with {C:\\}, {C::}, a part that ends
in a dot or a space, or a reserved name. Compare paths without case for
collisions. The fix of finding 13 closes the case of the destination on all
systems.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-34: Finding 34. chown after the xattrs removes file capabilities (information)}

{b:Where:} {C:writeFile} in {C bc=/._:src/internal/archive/extract.go}: the
xattrs, then {C:fchown}, then {C:fchmod}. Code only.

{b:Problem.} On Linux, {C:fchown} of a regular file clears
{C:security.capability}. So {C:-p --preserve-owner} as root never restores a
file capability, which design {ref t=design.html#metadata-extraction:§7.7}
says it does. {C:--diff-extract} as root reports a false difference for each
such file. The failure is safe: a capability is lost, never added.

{b:Fix.} The owner first, then the xattrs, then the mode. Test it in the CI
job that runs as root.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-35: Finding 35. The creation time in the header is not authenticated (information)}

{b:Where:} {C bc=/._:src/internal/format/header.go}; the keyed index digest
covers the uuid, the generation and the index, not the header. Tested.

{b:Problem.} With no passphrase, an attacker can set the creation time of an
encrypted archive. {C:--info} shows the new time, and {C:--verify} passes.
Design {ref t=design.html#integrity-its-limits:§14.4} names only the trailer
flags as a field without authentication. The uuid is bound into each key,
and a change of the other fields breaks the read.

{b:Fix.} Name the creation time in {ref t=design.html#integrity-its-limits:§14.4}.
In a later format version, bind the header into the input of the index
digest.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-36: Finding 36. A rollback needs no old copy (information)}

{b:Where:} the append path of {C bc=/._:src/internal/archive/writer.go}.
Tested.

{b:Problem.} Design {ref t=design.html#integrity-its-limits:§14.4} documents
a rollback as putting back an older archive. But an append writes after the
old trailer, so the current file holds each earlier generation until a
compact. To cut the file after an earlier trailer is enough: the archive
opens, {C:--verify} passes, and deleted or replaced members come back. A
{C:--repair} after damage at the end does the same.

{b:Fix.} Say in {ref t=design.html#integrity-its-limits:§14.4} and the man
page that a truncation is a rollback, and that only {C:--compact} removes the
earlier generations. The defence stays the same: record the uuid and the
generation in another place.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-37: Finding 37. After an append, the data key is not set to zero (information)}

{b:Where:} {C:OpenAppend}, {C:Writer.Close} and {C:Writer.abort} in
{C bc=/._:src/internal/archive/writer.go}. Code only.

{b:Problem.} The writer borrows the keys of the reader, and no code sets them
to zero after {C:-r}, {C:-u} or {C:--delete}. Design
{ref t=design.html#keys-passphrases:§14.2} says that the reader sets the data
key to zero when it closes. Create, compact and the change of passphrase do.

{b:Fix.} Call {C:Keys.Zero} in {C:Writer.Close} and {C:Writer.abort} for a
writer that {C:OpenAppend} made.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-38: Finding 38. --json writes C1 and text-direction characters as they are (information)}

{b:Where:} the JSON output in {C bc=/._:src/internal/cli/ops.go}. Tested.

{b:Problem.} {C:encoding/json} escapes C0 characters, but not U+0080 to
U+009F or the text-direction characters U+202A to U+202E and U+2066 to
U+2069. Design {ref t=design.html#names-terminal:§10.13} says that {C:--json}
escapes each name. On a terminal, a crafted name can show as another name.

{b:Fix.} Write these characters as {C:\\uXXXX} after the encode, which is
valid JSON, or correct the design.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-39: Finding 39. With -h, a link changed to a FIFO after the walk makes the open wait (information)}

{b:Where:} {C:osSrcPaths.Open} in {C bc=/._:src/internal/archive/src.go}, which
opens with no {C:O_NONBLOCK}. Tested through a test hook.

{b:Problem.} A user who can write the tree points a link at a file, then at a
FIFO after the walk. A {C:-h} backup waits for ever, with no message. {C:-h}
follows links by design ({ref t=design.html#archiving-tree-that-other:§14.5}),
so the effect is a hang only.

{b:Fix.} Open with {C:O_RDONLY} and {C:O_NONBLOCK}, as the other opens of the
walk do.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-40: Finding 40. No signal handling (information)}

{b:Where:} the program has no {C:signal.Notify}.
{C bc=/._:src/internal/cli/password_unix.go} restores the terminal in a
{C:defer}. Tested (the temporary file); Code (the prompt). Two parts of this
review found the prompt case separately.

{b:Problem.} {C:SIGINT} or {C:SIGTERM} during a create leaves the temporary
file {C:.NAME.create-*}, with the mode of the old archive and a part of the
archive, in the clear unless {C:-e}. A later walk of that directory stores
it. A compact leaves {C:.NAME.rewrite-*}. Ctrl-C at the passphrase prompt
ends the process before the {C:defer}, so echo stays off.

{b:Fix.} Catch {C:SIGINT}, {C:SIGTERM} and {C:SIGHUP}: restore the terminal,
remove the temporary file, and exit with 130 or 143.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-41: Finding 41. Windows: -f follows junctions, and a rewrite drops the ACL (information)}

{b:Where:} {C:MayFollow} in {C bc=/._:src/internal/meta/meta_windows.go}, and
the rewrite in {C bc=/._:src/internal/archive/mutate.go}. Code only.

{b:Problem.} On Windows, {C:checkLinks} does nothing, so a junction that
another user made on the path is followed, and the rename replaces its
target. Compact, the change of passphrase and {C:--encrypt-archive} write a
new file that inherits the ACL of the directory, so an explicit ACL on the
archive is lost. Design {ref t=design.html#other-platforms:§15.1} says that
modes are not kept, but does not name the ACL.

{b:Fix.} Copy the DACL of the old file before the rename. Refuse a reparse
point in {C:-f} that the caller does not own, or document the case.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-42: Finding 42. Shared content allows a ratio of 70,000 to 1 at extraction (information)}

{b:Where:} shared content, {ref t=design.html#identical-content-m11:§4.3}.
Tested.

{b:Problem.} This is the feature as designed: each member that shares
content is written in full. A plain archive of 1.4 MB lists 105 GB. With an
owner of 1 GiB of zeros, the same index gives about 100 TB. Design
{ref t=design.html#extraction-reads-disk:§14.3} does not name this cost
beside the decompression bombs.

{b:Fix.} Document it in {ref t=design.html#extraction-reads-disk:§14.3}. The
user sees the total in {C:-tv} and {C:--info} before an extraction. An option
such as {C:--max-extract-bytes}, or a check of free space against the
selected total, lets a script bound it.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-43: Finding 43. An unknown codec name gives exit 4 (information)}

{b:Where:} {C:ErrUnknownCodec} in {C bc=/._:src/internal/codec/codec.go}, and
{C:exitCodeFor} in {C bc=/._:src/internal/cli/run.go}. Code only.

{b:Problem.} An archive that names a codec that this build does not have is
"too new or damaged", which is exit 3 ({ref t=design.html#exit-codes:§10.7}).
The error has no mapping, so it gives exit 4, an I/O error.

{b:Fix.} Map {C:codec.ErrUnknownCodec} to exit 3, with a test.

{section 2 nc bid=finding-44: Finding 44. A member id can be used again after a compact (information)}

{b:Where:} {C:OpenAppend} in {C bc=/._:src/internal/archive/writer.go}. Code
only.

{b:Problem.} {C:--compact} drops a tombstone with the highest id, and the next
append takes the highest id plus one. So an id comes back. format.md §8.3
says that an id is never used again. Each new blob has a new salt and key, so
this has no effect on the cryptography.

{b:Fix.} Keep the highest id ever used, for example in the index, or correct
format.md §8.3.

