{section 1 bid=what-review-found-sound: What the review found sound}

{list t=*:
  {item: {b:Nonces and keys.} A test read every index of 22 generations from two full life cycles: create with a trained dictionary, {C:-u}, {C:-r}, {C:--delete}, compact, recompress, change of passphrase and {C:--encrypt-archive}. It found 1475 salts, and none sealed two different ciphertexts. The HKDF info strings cannot collide, no key has two purposes, and the wrap binds the archive uuid.}
  {item: {b:What the AADs bind.} A chunk binds the member id, its index and the last-chunk flag, which the reader takes from the authenticated index, so truncation and reorder fail. The index binds the generation, and its key binds the uuid. A dictionary binds its id. The review found no gap.}
  {item: {b:Index authentication.} In an encrypted archive, the keyed index digest is checked first, in constant time, before any decompression or decode. A member without a seal in an encrypted archive is refused, and the reverse fails.}
  {item: {b:KDF limits.} A crafted crypto header with time 65, or memory 2{S:32}-1, is refused with exit 3 before the prompt.}
  {item: {b:Parsing.} The header, trailer and crypto header are checked before any allocation that their numbers decide. {C:Validate} covers ids, types, sparse maps, the chunk table, shared content, hardlinks and dictionaries with no overflow. No input made the program panic.}
  {item: {b:Decoders.} The zstd window, the s2 length, the bound of gzip, flate and xz, the bytes after a stream, and the dictionary size all hold. 33,281 changed dictionaries given to the zstd encoder made no panic and no failed round trip.}
  {item: {b:Extraction.} {C:..}, absolute paths and links that lead out are refused. Pipes and devices use {C:*at} calls with one name. Temporary files are random, {C:O_EXCL} and 0600, and removed on error. A hardlink links only to a file of this run. The umask, setuid and xattr rules hold. {C:--strip-components} cannot give an empty path. The dry run makes nothing.}
  {item: {b:The scratch directory of {C:--diff-extract}.} It is made by {C:os.MkdirTemp} with mode 0700, all work in it is by unique names through {C:os.Root}, and it is removed at the end. Another user in {C:/tmp} cannot enter it.}
  {item: {b:The walk.} Each directory is held, each file is opened by one name, and its device and inode are checked. FIFOs and devices are never opened. Spill files hold sealed chunks only, are 0600, and are removed at once.}
  {item: {b:Locks and the write sequence.} The lock is taken before the trailer is read, and the path must still name the locked file. An append writes its index before its trailer, and an abort cuts the file back.}
  {item: {b:Configuration.} No file is read from the current directory. The owner and mode of the configuration file are checked on the open file. Passphrase keys are refused in it. {C:--no-config} ignores the file and the environment.}
  {item: {b:Messages.} Each error and warning, and the lines of {C:-t}, {C:-tv}, {C:-v}, {C:-n} and {C:--diff}, escape names on a terminal (except findings 25 and 38).}
}

{section 1 bid=order-of-work: Order of work}

The review suggests this order, from the effect on a user and the size of the
fix:

{list t=1:
  {item: Findings 13 and 14 together: one resolution of member paths with no link following, also for the metadata of the last phase. Correct design {ref t=design.html#extraction-reads-disk:§14.3}.}
  {item: Findings 15, 16 and 17 together: one resolution of {C:-f} in a single pass, with the operations relative to the parent directory, and the rule of {C:fs.protected_regular} for an existing file.}
  {item: Finding 18: a usage error. A small change.}
  {item: Finding 19: parameters as integers and strings only, and a bound on the decoded memory.}
  {item: Finding 20, then the other low findings. Findings 25, 31, 32, 37, 39 and 43 are each a small change.}
  {item: The documentation findings: 26, 27, 28, 35, 36, 42 and 44.}
}

Each fix needs a test that fails without it, as for the first review.
